===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} That which [[#Verb|bind]]s or [[tie]]s.
# {{senseid|en|Q123264650}} {{lb|en|countable}} A [[troublesome]] situation; a [[problem]]; a [[predicament]] or [[quandary]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:difficult situation}}
# Any [[twining]] or [[climbing]] [[plant]] or [[stem]], especially a [[hop]] vine; a [[bine]].
# {{lb|en|music|countable}} A [[ligature]] or [[tie]] for grouping [[note]]s.
# {{lb|en|chess|countable}} A [[strong]] [[grip]] or [[stranglehold]] on a position, which is [[difficult]] for the [[opponent]] to break.
#: {{co|en|the Maróczy '''Bind'''}}
# The [[indurated]] [[clay]] of [[coal mine]]s, or other overlying substances such as [[sandstone]] or [[shale]].

====Usage notes====
The phrase ''to see the bind that someone is in'' ({{senseno|en|Q123264650}}) has evolved in the 21st century, such that it is now just as often a [[sarcasm#Noun|sarcastic]] joke as not: it feigns sympathy in a situation that deserves little or no sympathy. Thus, for example, ''the company could avoid doing shortsighted things that undermine its long-term prospects, but if it did then today's stock price would be slightly lower, so you can see the bind they're in.''

